    "title": "London Easter bank holiday - Transport, shops, attractions",
    "text": "London will move to bank holiday hours this Easter, with shops closing and transport running reduced services. Easter means a shift in routine for Londoners as shops run reduced hours and public transport services change from normal running times. Shop opening hours over Easter On days such as Good Friday, April 3, and Easter Monday, April 6, larger shops in London will operate under \"Sunday trading\" rules. But on Easter Sunday, shoppers won't be able to rely on big supermarkets as they will be closed for the day, alongside most big shops in England. But smaller convenience stores across the city can open as usual. Good Friday, April 3, and Easter Monday, April 6, will see most shops opening on reduced hours. Expect West End flagships and shopping centres to open around 10am with earlier closing hours. The operation of supermarkets depends on location, but the general rule is reduced hours for bigger stores on Good Friday and Easter Monday, and closures on Easter Sunday. Small stores across the capital and high streets will have flexibility to open at usual times. On Easter Saturday, business is expected to operate as usual, keeping in line with typical Saturday late closing times in central London. Supermarket closures and rules Sunday service will mean that big supermarkets including Tesco, Sainsbury's, Asda, Aldi, Lidl, Morrisons, and M&S Food Halls will remain closed due to law restrictions. Several brands have already confirmed full Sunday closure for UK stores. Smaller chains and independent convenience stores will have the ability to open but may reduce working hours. Some of the variations in opening hours across stores is listed below, but you must always check your local store before making the trip. Lidl: open normal-style hours Good Friday and Saturday, closed Easter Sunday, reduced hours Easter Monday (around 9am–9pm). Aldi: open Good Friday and Saturday with long hours, closed Easter Sunday, shorter hours Easter Monday (around 8am–8pm). Tesco/Morrisons/Sainsbury’s/Asda/Waitrose/M&S: large stores closed Easter Sunday, with reduced or location‑specific hours on Good Friday and Easter Monday. Transport changes across London Transport services across London will move to weekend or bank holiday timetables from April 3 to April 6. This will be particularly true for tube, Overground, DLR, and buses. Some planned closures can be expected within different lines and branches due to ongoing engineering works. Commuters who rely on National Rail services may want to take note of the Easter bank holiday schedule. A list of engineering works is planned, affecting routes into and through London. London Waterloo will see reduced services, with train replacements on certain stretches of the Network Rail. The specific line-by-line plan for TFL will be available closer to the Easter weekend. Londoners can always check the TFL website, app, and National Rail Enquiries before planning their journey. Attractions and places to visit London's top attractions, such as London Eye, Tower of London, sightseeing bus tours, and Madame Tussauds are geared to remain operational throughout the Easter weekend. Museum-goers can look forward to Natural History Museum and Science Museum being open, with special Easter events and workshops for children. However, some venues might vary their usual hours. The British Museum, for instance, has been known to close earlier on Good Friday. Westminster Abbey and other churches and abbeys will prioritise religious services over tourism, specifically on Good Friday and Easter Sunday. For these venues, opening hours may fluctuate and only worship services may be available at exclusive times. Throughout this period, always remember to confirm timings via the website of the attraction before seizing your plans. What to expect overall Given the short notice closures or alterations, it’s best to be cautious. For London dwellers, the prolonged bank holiday feels like an extended Sunday, spreading over four days. Reduced train services, a day devoid of shopping at large supermarkets, but with the silver lining of operating high streets, markets, and major attractions. The atmosphere will be less of a complete shutdown and more of a continued festive hum, keeping the city alive with activity.",
